The 32-header cap taken in arrival order rather than over name-sorted order,
observed in nine SDKs. Captured by reverting only the Node fix in
sdk/reproit-backend-node/instrument.js (boundedHeaders) and running the scoped
regression command against the landed vector test.

$ node --test --test-name-pattern "header vectors" \
    sdk/reproit-backend-node/test/behavior-vectors.test.js

✖ header vectors (1.545875ms)
ℹ tests 1
ℹ suites 0
ℹ pass 0
ℹ fail 1
ℹ cancelled 0
ℹ skipped 0
ℹ todo 0

✖ failing tests:

test at sdk/reproit-backend-node/test/behavior-vectors.test.js:86:1
✖ header vectors
  AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: the cap must be over sorted names, not the
  order the headers arrived in

  'x-h39' !== 'x-h31'

exit status 1

The same defect, in the same words, in the other eight:

  reproit-backend-py       _bounded_headers  list(items)[:32] before sorted()
  reproit-backend-rs       bounded_headers   .take(32) on the raw iterator
  reproit-backend-java     boundedHeaders    LinkedHashMap, arrival order
  reproit-backend-dotnet   BoundedHeaders    List<KeyValuePair>, arrival order
  reproit-backend-rb       bounded_headers   .first(32) on the raw hash
  reproit-backend-php      bounded_headers   array_slice before ksort
  reproit-react-native     boundedHeaders    Object.entries(...).slice(0, 32)
  reproit-android          boundedHeaders    entries.take(32), no sort
  reproit-backend-go       boundedHeaders    sorted the wire spelling, not the
                                             recorded lowercase name

Go was the SDK the vector was originally written against, in 2026, after its
randomized map iteration recorded a different header subset on every run. The
vector describing sorted order then sat in sdk/capture-behavior-v1.json while
nine more SDKs shipped the same defect, because only one SDK executed the
headers group.
